WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.226
"Boosting Anticancer Immunity
with Forest Bathing"

00:00:07.302 --> 00:00:12.120
Previously, I showed how exposure to
nature can have self-reported

00:00:12.120 --> 00:00:14.880
psychological benefits, but
there was a dearth of data

00:00:14.880 --> 00:00:19.560
on changes in objective measurements;
so, I was excited to see this paper

00:00:19.560 --> 00:00:24.120
on the effects on levels of the
stress hormone cortisol in the saliva

00:00:24.120 --> 00:00:27.800
of those partaking in
“forest bathing,” which just means

00:00:27.800 --> 00:00:31.040
visiting a forest and
surrounding yourself by trees.

00:00:31.040 --> 00:00:34.520
The level of cortisol in your
saliva is considered an indicator

00:00:34.520 --> 00:00:37.400
of your stress level, and
after walking in a forest,

00:00:37.400 --> 00:00:40.320
compared to walking in a city,
or even after just hanging out

00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:43.920
in a forest compared to a city,
people’s salivary cortisol levels

00:00:43.920 --> 00:00:49.280
were significantly lower. But wait&nbsp;
a second, the same effect was found

00:00:49.280 --> 00:00:52.040
before they went to the forest. Huh?

00:00:52.040 --> 00:00:55.560
Forest bathing was associated with
significantly lower salivary cortisol

00:00:55.560 --> 00:00:59.760
both before and after, compared
with visiting an urban area.

00:00:59.760 --> 00:01:03.200
Therefore, it appears that just the
thought of going to spend the day

00:01:03.200 --> 00:01:05.120
in the forest relieved stress.

00:01:05.120 --> 00:01:09.400
So, when comparing the effects of
forest bathing versus urban visiting,

00:01:09.400 --> 00:01:13.760
the anticipation placebo effect
may play a more important role

00:01:13.760 --> 00:01:16.520
in influencing stress levels
than the actual experience

00:01:16.520 --> 00:01:19.520
of being in the forest.
So, I was ready to dismiss it

00:01:19.520 --> 00:01:25.600
as just another nebulous psychological
effect until...I read this.

00:01:25.600 --> 00:01:28.880
Studies on the effects of forest
bathing on the immune function

00:01:28.880 --> 00:01:33.120
showed that visiting a forest
can induce a significant increase

00:01:33.120 --> 00:01:36.000
in the number and activity
of natural killer cells,

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:39.400
one of the ways our
body fights off cancer.

00:01:39.400 --> 00:01:43.920
That got my attention.
It all started with this study.

00:01:43.920 --> 00:01:47.560
Twelve men were taken on a long
weekend to walk in some forests,

00:01:47.560 --> 00:01:50.600
and almost all of the subjects
(11 out of 12) showed higher

00:01:50.600 --> 00:01:54.440
natural killer cell activity after
the trip, and not just a little—

00:01:54.440 --> 00:01:57.560
about a 50% increase
compared to before the trip.

00:01:57.560 --> 00:02:00.400
Now, just exercise can
affect immune function,

00:02:00.400 --> 00:02:02.600
but they weren’t walking any extra;

00:02:02.600 --> 00:02:06.120
they were just walking
in a forest instead.

00:02:06.120 --> 00:02:08.400
Yeah, but they also were
taken on a trip somewhere,

00:02:08.400 --> 00:02:09.760
introducing other variables.

00:02:09.760 --> 00:02:12.400
So, how about randomizing
them to go on

00:02:12.400 --> 00:02:15.240
some city trip versus the forest trip.

00:02:15.240 --> 00:02:17.640
And if there is some
special forest effect,

00:02:17.640 --> 00:02:19.360
how long does the effect last?

00:02:19.360 --> 00:02:21.880
Do you have to like walk
in the forest every day?

00:02:21.880 --> 00:02:25.080
Before jumping into all that,
how about we first see

00:02:25.080 --> 00:02:27.680
if it works in women too.

00:02:27.680 --> 00:02:30.960
Same kind of set-up, and same
results: a significant boost

00:02:30.960 --> 00:02:33.760
in natural killer cell activity
walking around in the woods.

00:02:33.760 --> 00:02:37.760
And this time they went back a
week later to retest them,

00:02:37.760 --> 00:02:41.720
and they were still up, though
after a month they came back down.

00:02:41.720 --> 00:02:47.560
But hey, once a week should do it,
but it was a multiple-day trip.

00:02:47.560 --> 00:02:50.760
Who has time to hang out in
forests all weekend, every weekend?

00:02:50.760 --> 00:02:55.200
How about just a little day trip?
The title gives it all away.

00:02:55.200 --> 00:03:00.360
Boom! Same thing! The same big jump
measured the day after the trip

00:03:00.360 --> 00:03:03.320
compared to before and with
the same staying power.

00:03:03.320 --> 00:03:07.000
Natural killer cell activity
still boosted a week later.

00:03:07.000 --> 00:03:10.080
This suggests that if people
visit a suburban forest park

00:03:10.080 --> 00:03:14.040
once a week on a day trip,
they may be able to maintain

00:03:14.040 --> 00:03:17.320
the increased anti-cancer immunity.

00:03:17.320 --> 00:03:21.560
OK, but I’m still not convinced.
How can you attribute the benefit

00:03:21.560 --> 00:03:26.080
to the forest itself, when all
you have is before and after data?

00:03:26.080 --> 00:03:28.560
To make the case that nature
had anything to do with it

00:03:28.560 --> 00:03:32.080
you’d need a control group
that took the same kind of trip

00:03:32.080 --> 00:03:36.800
but went to somewhere else instead.
And… here we go.

00:03:36.800 --> 00:03:40.280
It turns out visiting a
forest, but not a city,

00:03:40.280 --> 00:03:43.280
increases human natural killer activity.

00:03:43.280 --> 00:03:45.760
Here’s the forest data just like before,

00:03:45.760 --> 00:03:50.000
but nothing on a trip to
go walking in a city.

00:03:50.000 --> 00:03:51.080
By the end of the forest trip,

00:03:51.080 --> 00:03:55.600
the subjects experienced a boost of 80%
in NK activity after forest bathing,

00:03:55.600 --> 00:03:58.240
compared to only a 10% bump
for the city walkers.

00:03:58.240 --> 00:04:01.920
And both trips were matched for
physical activity, and alcohol,

00:04:01.920 --> 00:04:04.520
and sleep—other things that
can affect immune function.

00:04:04.520 --> 00:04:08.560
And so, here we go.
Confirmation of boosted immunity,

00:04:08.560 --> 00:04:11.840
but only on the forest trip,
“indicating that forest bathing

00:04:11.840 --> 00:04:15.760
does indeed enhance [natural
killer cell] activity.”

00:04:15.760 --> 00:04:18.200
Moreover, they found that
the increased activity lasted

00:04:18.200 --> 00:04:21.600
up to 30 days after the
trip. Check it out.

00:04:21.600 --> 00:04:25.440
Still up a week later and
even a bit up a month later.

00:04:25.440 --> 00:04:29.480
“This suggests that if people
visit a forest once a month,

00:04:29.480 --> 00:04:34.960
they may be able to maintain increased
[natural killer cell] activity.”

00:04:34.960 --> 00:04:38.240
OK, so, now that we know
that it’s a real effect,

00:04:38.240 --> 00:04:40.080
the next question is, why?

00:04:40.760 --> 00:04:44.080
What is it about forests
that gives us the boost?

00:04:44.080 --> 00:04:49.920
And (you can imagine Big Pharma
thinking) can you make it into a pill?

00:04:49.920 --> 00:04:53.808
We’ll find out next.

